### D18 — Camera frames the group, fit-first split, rigs, death spectate (revised 2026-10-30)

The camera is part of the read, not a fixed follow. All numbers live in `CAMERA` in `config.ts`.
`src/game/camera.ts` owns the rig director; the renderer owns framing, grouping and viewports.

- **Group framing**: every local pane anchors on the bounding-box centre of the local players in
  that pane, not on one avatar. Other live players, the training drone, worms, viruses, sentries and
  cascading-error blocks inside `CAMERA.focusRange` pull the frame by a distance-weighted mean offset,
  capped at `CAMERA.maxBias`; the pull skips the pane's own locals. The anchor is then clamped to the
  middle band (`CAMERA.edgeMargin`); an arena bound can still pin the camera first.
- **Fit first, then split**: local players stay on one screen while their whole group fits at
  `CAMERA.sharedZoomMin` (0.68x the base, with `CAMERA.groupPad` around the box). Only when that
  maximum pull-back cannot hold everyone does the view split, and then into the smallest number of
  panes: the farthest pair peels apart repeatedly until each group fits a pane. Four players with one
  across the arena become two panes, not four; a group of three shares a pane. `CAMERA.shareSlack` is
  the merge hysteresis. One pane is full screen, two side by side, three or four on the 2x2 grid.
  - Do not replace this with a fixed pairwise distance. The fit is the design rule; a wide pair can
    legitimately share a screen, and a tight trio plus one outlier should not cost three extra panes.
  - A dead local is a spectator and does not count toward the fit; a dead body across the arena must
    not split the survivors' screen.
- **Dynamic zoom**: `CAMERA.zoomMin` and `zoomMax` are the combat multipliers; a pane additionally
  uses `CAMERA.splitZoom`. A single shared view may pull past combat zoom to `sharedZoomMin` to fit
  the group. The exact cap also includes `CAMERA.groupPad`, so an avatar is never on the pane edge.
  Zoom eases separately (`zoomFollow`). Kit numbers stay in world units ... 
- **Pane frames and HUD**: each split pane has a 1.6px outline in its primary local player's color,
  over a dark underlay, plus a faint inner line. The outline is what makes two similar floors read as
  two windows. Readiness docks are per local player at screen corners: top-left for a solo view, then
  top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right as locals join. One shared minimap serves the whole screen:
  lower-left for a solo view, top-centre whenever the view is split or more than one local is active,
  so it does not belong to one pane. It shows every local player and all threat families.
- **Layout slow motion**: when the pane count or grouping changes, `layoutChanged` is raised once; the
  local sim dips to `layoutSlowScale` (0.35) for `layoutSlowTime` (0.55 s) and eases back. Network
  play and the stress bench never slow. The camera keeps moving at real speed while the sim dips; the
  point is to give the eye time to find the avatar before a shot lands.
- **Rig director**: `CameraDirector` holds four reusable rigs (`CameraRig`: world point or
  `followSlot`, zoom multiplier, hold/in/out, weight, priority). The highest priority wins; it blends
  over the default framing and hands control back on expiry. `Renderer.playCameraRig` is the API for a
  boss entrance or a narrative beat. Shipped cues: a gauntlet wave glances at the nearest sentry pack
  outside `CAMERA.waveLookAhead`; the campaign breach pushes in 8%. Keep cues short and opt-in; a full
  blend may leave the avatar off frame on purpose.
- **Split scope**: ... QA hooks ...
- **Death spectate**: unchanged text...
